  "key_findings": [
    "MF2-GARCH does NOT significantly outperform GJR-GARCH(1,1) in OOS QLIKE for any asset tested. The complexity ceiling holds.",
    "Best tau specification is consistently MA(126) for equities, suggesting 6-month realized variance captures the long-run trend better than shorter windows.",
    "Interesting trade-off: MF2 with longer MA windows produces BETTER MSE and Mincer-Zarnowitz R² (calibration) but WORSE QLIKE. This suggests the two-component structure helps with level estimation but hurts with tail/asymmetric loss.",
    "MF2 alpha systematically collapses to ~0 for equity assets (SPY, QQQ, EEM), meaning the short-run ARCH effect vanishes when tau captures the slow-moving level. Only gamma (leverage) remains active.",
    "GLD is the only asset where MF2 wins (marginally, QLIKE ratio=1.0005), possibly because GLD has no leverage effect (gamma=0), making the tau-g decomposition cleaner.",
    "No DM test is significant at any conventional level. The QLIKE differences are economically and statistically trivial.",
    "This aligns with our Complexity Ceiling Score finding (K22): single-component GJR-GARCH is hard to beat with added complexity."
  ],
  "implications_for_research": [
    "MF2-GARCH does not warrant addition to the model table or strategy pipeline.",
    "The MSE vs QLIKE trade-off is interesting from an academic perspective: two-component models may be better for point forecasts (MSE/R²) but worse for density/VaR forecasts (QLIKE).",
    "For VT strategies that only need the direction of volatility (high/low regime), MF2's tau component is essentially an MA of squared returns — already captured by EWMA or 12/VIX.",
    "The alpha→0 phenomenon suggests that for equities with strong leverage, the short-run ARCH effect is subsumed by the long-run trend tracker."
  ]
